  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Concrete and well-scoped portable identity profile. ICR makes signing-key continuity auditable across browser/helper embodiments and rotations, EC captures constrained-host limits, and SAE keeps off-grid live behavior out of claimed conformance. Main gaps are browser-storage/XSS guidance, break-witness sufficiency, and some still-open interop details.",
    "strengths": [
      "ICR gives explicit old-to-new key linkage and cross-embodiment continuity evidence.",
      "EC makes host-specific limits and excluded guarantees auditable instead of implied.",
      "AM/EC/ICR/SAE cleanly separate app identity, signing identity, and durable publication.",
      "Local ledgers plus break-witnesses support later audit from partial evidence."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Browser key storage and XSS risk are only indirectly addressed.",
      "Handshake and crypto-agility details remain intentionally provisional.",
      "Witness requirements when both keys cannot sign are still underspecified."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Continuity receipts could be read as stronger security than browser or host reality justifies.",
      "Weak break-witness policy could leave disputed continuity after loss or compromise.",
      "Multiple SAE cited-object variants may fragment interop unless narrowed."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What minimum guide text should describe browser key storage limits and XSS risk?",
      "What witness or break-witness threshold is sufficient when co-signing is impossible?",
      "Which algorithm, rotation, and handshake fields must be explicit for a pivotable v0 profile?",
      "Should SAE normalize one cited-object shape before broader variant support?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  },
